# Headers for the paths the Worker never sees.
#
# Until 2026-09-07 `run_worker_first = ["/*", "!/assets/*"]` in wrangler.toml
# kept the hashed bundles on the asset server's fast path, and they skipped
# `harden()` in worker/index.ts — so this file was the bundles' ONLY `nosniff`.
# It is `run_worker_first = true` now (audit item 39): the negation was matched
# against the path, not against what existed at it, so a MISS under `/assets/`
# was the SPA fallback shell with no security headers and this file's year-long
# `immutable`. Every response now passes through `harden()`; the Worker still
# reads the bundles through the asset binding, which still applies this file,
# so the cache policy below is what a real bundle hit carries and `nosniff` is
# set twice. Both stay: Pages is the rollback and has no Worker.
#
# Do not reintroduce a negation, however narrow. A miss can be named anything.
#
# This is read as configuration by Workers static assets, the same way
# `_redirects` is — which is why `_redirects` cannot simply be deleted and has to
# be stripped from dist/ at deploy time. Unlike that file this one is valid for
# both hosts, so it needs no stripping: Pages applies it too, and Pages is still
# the rollback.
#
# The immutable cache header is safe only because these filenames carry a content
# hash: a changed file is a changed URL, so nothing can go stale.
/assets/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two asset families Vite does NOT hash, because they are referenced by a
# fixed path rather than imported (`fonts.css` names `/fonts/…`, and the photo
# tiles name `/photos/…`). With no rule here they took the asset server's
# default — `max-age=0, must-revalidate` — so **192KB of webfonts and 2.0MB of
# photographs were revalidated on every cold navigation**, while the 512KB
# bundle beside them was `immutable`. That is a conditional request per file per
# page view, on the slow connections the copy is written for.
#
# **A week, not a year, and deliberately not `immutable`.** These filenames
# carry no content hash, so a changed file is the *same* URL — the property that
# makes `/assets/*` safe above is exactly what these lack. A week bounds the
# staleness of a re-cut font or a replaced photograph to something the operator
# can wait out, while removing essentially every revalidation inside a real
# visit. `stale-while-revalidate` then takes the round trip off the critical
# path for the week after that.
#
# If these ever need to be truly immutable, the fix is to move them under `src/`
# so Vite content-hashes them into `/assets/` and they inherit the rule above —
# not to lengthen the figure here. `docs/FONTS.md` is the ledger either way.
/fonts/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=604800

/photos/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, stale-while-revalidate=604800
